  "analysis": "The patient presents with facial swelling progressing to total body swelling, proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and fatty casts in urine, indicative of nephrotic syndrome. These findings are classic for minimal change disease in children. The absence of hypertension, hematuria, and recent infection makes acute glomerulonephritis less likely. Congestive heart failure typically presents with other signs like respiratory distress and hepatomegaly. Kwashiorkor is characterized by protein-energy malnutrition with a different clinical presentation. Protein-losing enteropathy usually presents with diarrhea and malabsorption. Therefore, minimal change disease is the most likely etiology in this case.",
